  "bank": "kangaroo",
  "source": {
   "contest": "Canadian Math Kangaroo",
   "paper": "Grade 7-8",
   "year": 2013,
   "number": 27,
   "url": "https://api.mathkangaroo.ca/api/v1/media/2013/2013gr0708e.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "7-8",
  "points": 5,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "Nick, Ryan, Simon and James from the band Dandelion sang a few songs at their first concert. Always three of them were singing, and one was playing the guitar in accompaniment. James sang the most songs of all of them – 8. Ryan sang 5 songs, which was fewer than anyone else from the group. How many songs did Dandelion sing at their first concert?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "8"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "9"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "10"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "13"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "26"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "B",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Count singer-songs",
    "body": "Every song has 3 singers, so (total of everyone's song counts) $= 3 \\times$ (number of songs).\n\nNick and Simon each sang more than 5 and fewer than 8 songs (James sang the most), so each sang 6 or 7. The total is $8 + 5 + (6 \\text{ or } 7) + (6 \\text{ or } 7)$, i.e. 25, 26 or 27. Only 27 is divisible by 3.\n\nSo there were $27 \\div 3 = 9$ songs.\n\n**Answer: (B) 9**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Count the guitar instead",
    "body": "In each song exactly one member plays guitar. With $T$ songs, a member who sang $s$ songs played guitar $T - s$ times, and these add up to $T$:\n\n$(T - 8) + (T - 5) + (T - n) + (T - s) = T$, so $3T = 13 + n + s$ with $n, s \\in \\{6, 7\\}$. Only $n = s = 7$ gives a multiple of 3: $3T = 27$, $T = 9$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 9**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "logic",
   "arithmetic"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
