 {
  "id": "kangaroo-ca-2013-g0708-30",
  "bank": "kangaroo",
  "source": {
   "contest": "Canadian Math Kangaroo",
   "paper": "Grade 7-8",
   "year": 2013,
   "number": 30,
   "url": "https://api.mathkangaroo.ca/api/v1/media/2013/2013gr0708e.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "7-8",
  "points": 5,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "A positive integer $N$ is smaller than the sum of its three greatest divisors (naturally, excluding $N$ itself). Which of the following statements is true?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "All such $N$ are divisible by 4."
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "All such $N$ are divisible by 5."
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "All such $N$ are divisible by 6."
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "All such $N$ are divisible by 7."
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "There is no such $N$."
   }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Divisors come in pairs",
    "body": "If $1 < a < b < c$ are the three smallest divisors of $N$ greater than 1, then the three greatest divisors below $N$ are $\\frac Na, \\frac Nb, \\frac Nc$. The condition is\n\n$$\\frac Na + \\frac Nb + \\frac Nc > N \\iff \\frac1a + \\frac1b + \\frac1c > 1.$$\n\n- If $a \\ge 3$, then $\\frac1a+\\frac1b+\\frac1c \\le \\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15 < 1$. So $a = 2$.\n- Then we need $\\frac1b + \\frac1c > \\frac12$. If $b \\ge 4$, $\\frac1b + \\frac1c \\le \\frac14 + \\frac15 < \\frac12$. So $b = 3$.\n\nHence $N$ is divisible by 2 and 3, i.e. by 6. Such $N$ exist, e.g. $N = 12$: $6 + 4 + 3 = 13 > 12$.\n\nCounterexamples to the other options: 6 itself fails (divisors below 6 are only 3, 2, 1, sum 6), but 12 works and is not divisible by 5 or 7; $N = 30$ ($15 + 10 + 6 = 31 > 30$) is not divisible by 4.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
